## the yoneda lemma for a functor `φ` on types: a natural transformation
## from
## each function out of `α` to `φ` is determined by one value of `α φ`, its
## image of `identity`, and every such value giveth one.
## a transformation from each function out of `α` to `φ`.
show let yoneda: [φ: [*, *], α: *, *] ≔ [@β: *, [α, β], β φ]

## `n` is natural when changing its outcome agreeth with changing its input.
show let natural: [@φ: [*, *], @: φ functor, @α: *, n: φ yoneda α, *]
  ≔ [@β: *, @γ: *, f: [α, β], g: [β, γ], ((f n) map g) = ((f ▷ g) n)]

## the transformation that a value giveth: tweak it by each function.
show let lift: [@φ: [*, *], @: φ functor, @α: *, a: α φ, φ yoneda α] ≔ a map

## the value that a transformation determineth: its image of `identity`.
show let unlift: [@φ: [*, *], @α: *, n: φ yoneda α, α φ] ≔ identity n

## lifting a value and taking it back giveth the value.
show let unlift_lift
  : [@φ: [*, *], @: φ functor, @α: *, a: α φ, ((a lift) unlift) = a]
  ≔ a map_identity

## a natural transformation is the lift of its own value, at every input.
show let lift_unlift
  : [@φ: [*, *], @: φ functor, @α: *, n: φ yoneda α, natural_n: n natural,
    @β: *, f: [α, β], (f ((n unlift) lift)) = (f n)]
  ≔ identity natural_n f
